1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,624
Welcome to our IETF 127
Hackathon demonstration:

2
00:00:05,664 --> 00:00:15,252
CoAP over Bundle Protocol version 7, with RFC
9758 IPN addressing and CoAP message aggregation.

3
00:00:15,792 --> 00:00:21,223
This work is championed onsite by
Professor Anna Calveras, and remotely by

4
00:00:21,263 --> 00:00:25,511
Mahitab S. Elattar, of the
Wireless Networks Group at UPC.

5
00:00:26,851 --> 00:00:33,330
Our implementation follows
draft-ietf-core-coap-bp, version five.

6
00:00:33,370 --> 00:00:40,150
CoAP runs directly over BPv7, with
no CoAP-to-UDP proxy in the path.

7
00:00:40,690 --> 00:00:47,529
Every endpoint uses the three-element
IPN identifier defined in RFC 9758:

8
00:00:47,569 --> 00:00:50,262
allocator, node, and service.

9
00:00:50,802 --> 00:00:56,323
We tested two non-confirmable applications,
a Safety GET and a Science POST, first

10
00:00:56,363 --> 00:00:59,307
as single messages and then as an Aggregate.

11
00:00:59,847 --> 00:01:04,484
And we validated the results at packet
level, with a live capture alongside

12
00:01:04,524 --> 00:01:07,914
independent application logs
on Earth and on the Rover.

13
00:01:09,254 --> 00:01:12,036
The testbed has three virtual machines.

14
00:01:12,076 --> 00:01:15,220
The Rover, node one, hosts two applications:

15
00:01:15,260 --> 00:01:18,693
Safety on service 10, and
Science on service 11.

16
00:01:19,233 --> 00:01:24,845
The Satellite, node two, is a store-and-forward
relay that forwards BPv7 bundles.

17
00:01:25,385 --> 00:01:30,156
Earth, node three, exposes the
CoAP application on service 64.

18
00:01:30,696 --> 00:01:37,922
All three nodes share allocator 974848, and
they are linked with the TCP Convergence

19
00:01:37,962 --> 00:01:42,419
Layer, version 3, with µD3TN
forwarding the bundles.

20
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:48,482
This is our Mars Rover Mission Control
interface, captured during the test run.

21
00:01:49,022 --> 00:01:54,416
Here, the Science application sends one
Aggregate built from two CoAP POST requests.

22
00:01:54,956 --> 00:02:00,314
The Earth application receives both requests,
and the Rover matches each of the two

23
00:02:00,354 --> 00:02:01,875
responses independently:

24
00:02:01,915 --> 00:02:03,891
two out of two replies matched.

25
00:02:05,231 --> 00:02:13,881
On the Earth node, Wireshark 4.6.9 decodes
the live TCPCL version 3 stream, captured

26
00:02:13,921 --> 00:02:15,950
on interface enp0s8.

27
00:02:16,490 --> 00:02:18,775
The decode goes layer by layer:

28
00:02:18,815 --> 00:02:25,078
the convergence layer, the BPv7 bundle, and
then CoAP, using an application-specific

29
00:02:25,118 --> 00:02:26,803
CoAP over BP dissector.

30
00:02:27,343 --> 00:02:31,479
Frame 76, highlighted here,
carries the Aggregate request.

31
00:02:32,819 --> 00:02:37,919
Before aggregating, we established
a baseline with single messages.

32
00:02:37,959 --> 00:02:43,134
The Safety GET used a 59-byte BP
payload, and returned 2.05 Content.

33
00:02:43,674 --> 00:02:49,501
The single Science POST used a 102-byte
payload, and returned 2.01 Created.

34
00:02:50,041 --> 00:02:54,090
As expected, single messages do not
carry the Payload-length option.

35
00:02:55,430 --> 00:02:57,901
Frame 76 is the key evidence.

36
00:02:58,441 --> 00:03:08,263
One bundle travels from Rover Science,
at IPN 974848.1.11, to the Earth CoAP

37
00:03:08,303 --> 00:03:11,377
service, at 974848.3.64.

38
00:03:11,917 --> 00:03:19,956
Its BP payload is 212 bytes, and the dissector
identifies two separate CoAP POST messages inside it.

39
00:03:21,296 --> 00:03:27,332
Each member is an independent non-confirmable
POST, with its own 24-bit message ID

40
00:03:27,372 --> 00:03:28,666
and its own token.

41
00:03:29,206 --> 00:03:35,502
Member one and member two use consecutive message
IDs and distinct tokens, as shown on the right.

42
00:03:36,042 --> 00:03:41,262
Both members carry the experimental
Payload-length option, set to 50 bytes, so the

43
00:03:41,302 --> 00:03:44,020
receiver can delimit each member's payload.

44
00:03:45,360 --> 00:03:51,651
Wireshark also confirms the bundle lifetime
directly in the BPv7 primary block:

45
00:03:51,691 --> 00:03:54,456
90,000 milliseconds, or 90 seconds.

46
00:03:54,996 --> 00:04:01,011
This is exactly the configured non-confirmable
lifetime of 100 seconds, minus the

47
00:04:01,051 --> 00:04:03,702
10-second maximum aggregation delay.

48
00:04:04,242 --> 00:04:06,675
And the primary-block CRC check is good.

49
00:04:08,015 --> 00:04:14,481
The expanded endpoint fields independently
verify the RFC 9758 encoding.

50
00:04:14,521 --> 00:04:18,095
Scheme code 2 identifies the IPN scheme.

51
00:04:18,635 --> 00:04:24,127
An element count of 3 means that allocator,
node, and service are encoded separately.

52
00:04:24,667 --> 00:04:31,549
Both the source, node 1 service 11, and
the destination, node 3 service 64, use

53
00:04:31,589 --> 00:04:34,791
allocator 974848 with three elements.

54
00:04:36,131 --> 00:04:41,604
On Earth, the application parsed both
POST members, and generated two separate

55
00:04:41,644 --> 00:04:44,219
non-confirmable 2.01 Created replies.

56
00:04:44,759 --> 00:04:51,101
Each reply carries the token of its own request,
so the Rover matched both responses independently.

57
00:04:51,641 --> 00:04:54,968
The application report and
the Wireshark trace agree:

58
00:04:55,008 --> 00:04:57,699
two complete request-response interactions.

59
00:04:59,039 --> 00:05:02,937
In total, the run completed four
of four Safety GET interactions,

60
00:05:03,477 --> 00:05:05,902
and three of three Science POST interactions.

61
00:05:06,442 --> 00:05:10,488
Two of those Science POSTs
shared a single BPv7 Aggregate.

62
00:05:11,028 --> 00:05:12,676
To be clear about scope:

63
00:05:12,716 --> 00:05:17,179
this shows successful operation
in a connected three-VM testbed.

64
00:05:17,219 --> 00:05:22,315
It is not yet a claim about reliability
under long outages or congestion.

65
00:05:23,655 --> 00:05:26,178
Some open standards questions remain.

66
00:05:26,218 --> 00:05:31,928
Draft five sets NSTART to one by default,
while our controlled demo shows NSTART of

67
00:05:31,968 --> 00:05:36,708
two, so implementation enforcement
and congestion safety need review.

68
00:05:37,248 --> 00:05:43,096
The Payload-length option, number 65000,
is experimental, and its option bits do not

69
00:05:43,136 --> 00:05:46,741
yet match the draft's
critical and unsafe semantics.

70
00:05:47,281 --> 00:05:54,364
Our testing covered non-confirmable unicast GET
and POST only, with three-element IPN and no proxy.

71
00:05:54,904 --> 00:06:00,162
Next, we will compare Single and Aggregate
modes under scheduled contacts, link

72
00:06:00,202 --> 00:06:01,571
disruption, and load.

73
00:06:01,611 --> 00:06:06,803
This is functional implementation evidence,
not a full interoperability claim.

74
00:06:08,143 --> 00:06:09,826
Thank you for watching.

75
00:06:09,866 --> 00:06:13,046
We demonstrated a CoAP Aggregate over BPv7:

76
00:06:13,086 --> 00:06:17,914
two non-confirmable POSTs, one
bundle, and two matched responses.

77
00:06:18,454 --> 00:06:23,739
We warmly welcome implementation feedback
from the CoRE and DTN working groups, and

78
00:06:23,779 --> 00:06:25,984
from fellow Hackathon participants.
